// The block device class protocol (spec/ARCHITECTURE.md section 7): what
// every block driver provides, and what the block server provides again for
// each partition, so a filesystem cannot tell the two apart.

/// A block device: storage addressed in fixed-size blocks.
///
/// The calls here are the control path: what the device is, and setting up
/// the data path. Requests themselves do not travel as calls. They go
/// through a pair of rings in a frame the client shares (`connect`), and
/// their data lies in frames the client registers (`add_memory`) and the
/// device reads and writes in place: nothing is copied between the client
/// and the device. The layout of the rings and of the records in them is
/// part of this protocol; it is written out in the `silica_block` crate's
/// documentation, which also has a client.
///
/// Each capability to the device is one client, connected once; several
/// clients may be connected at a time (a disk's partitions, each through a
/// capability `narrow` made). What the provider keeps for a client is
/// charged to the program it was given to (a device manager's consumer:
/// `silica_protocols::driver_badge`), each program bounded on its own by
/// what the provider can keep: a share of what it holds for clients
/// (`silica_block::service::SHARE` capability slots: a window one, a
/// connection two, a registered frame two) and at most 16 connections;
/// past them `no_room`, and no program's use takes another's place
/// (version 2). Everything a client shares stays the client's: it takes the
/// rings and the memory back by revoking them
/// (or by ending), at which point the device can no longer reach them and
/// the provider treats the connection as closed. A provider that ends is
/// seen by its client as `peer_gone` on the next call here; requests it had
/// not completed are to be sent again to the provider that replaces it
/// (reads and writes of blocks may be repeated).
protocol silica.block.Device version 2 {
    /// Why a call was refused.
    enum Error : u32 {
        /// Another client is connected, or this one already is.
        busy = 1,
        /// `connect` has not been called.
        not_connected = 2,
        /// The capability is not a frame of a usable size, or the number
        /// is already in use (`add_memory`) or names nothing (`remove_memory`).
        bad_memory = 3,
        /// The provider has no room for more: memory or table space.
        no_room = 4,
        /// Requests that use the memory are still in progress.
        in_use = 5,
        /// The blocks asked for are not all within what this capability
        /// reaches.
        outside = 6,
        /// No traced request of this client's has that tag.
        no_such_request = 7,
    }

    /// What a block device is.
    struct Info {
        /// Bytes in a block: a power of two, at least 512.
        block_size: u32,
        /// Number of blocks.
        blocks: u64,
        /// Writes are refused.
        read_only: bool,
        /// The device has a volatile write cache: written data is durable
        /// only after a flush request completes. Without one every
        /// completed write is durable.
        write_cache: bool,
        /// Most blocks one request may cover.
        max_blocks: u32,
    }

    /// Describes the device. May be called at any time.
    info @1 () -> (info: Info);

    /// Sets up the data path. `rings` is one frame of 16 KiB holding the
    /// request ring and the completion ring; `completions` is signalled by
    /// the provider when it has added completions and the client said it
    /// was going to sleep. The result is the notification the client
    /// signals when it has added requests and the provider said it was
    /// going to sleep.
    connect @2 (rings: cap<Frame>, completions: cap<Notification>) -> (requests: cap<Notification>) error Error;

    /// Registers a frame of the client's as memory requests may use, under
    /// the number `id` (below 64). Requests name their data as a number, an
    /// offset and a length. The device is given the frame for reading, and
    /// with `writable` for writing too (needed for read requests).
    add_memory @3 (id: u32, frame: cap<Frame>, writable: bool) -> () error Error;

    /// Withdraws a registration. The device can no longer reach the frame
    /// when the call returns.
    remove_memory @4 (id: u32) -> () error Error;

    /// A capability to the same device that reaches only `blocks` blocks
    /// from block `first` of what this one reaches, numbered from 0 there:
    /// a partition. Whoever holds a capability may narrow it and hand the
    /// result on; the provider enforces the range on every request made
    /// through it, and `info` through it says `blocks` blocks. Each call
    /// makes a capability of its own, a client apart from every other,
    /// even of the same blocks: no holder reaches another's connection or
    /// memory (version 2; before, whoever narrowed the same range was given
    /// the same capability). Each is one slot of its program's share, and at
    /// most 4 lie under one capability narrowed from the whole device; past
    /// either, `no_room`. What a program has let go of (everything derived
    /// from it gone, its client disconnected, and the same for everything
    /// under it) makes room; no holder's capability is ever taken back to
    /// make room for another's.
    narrow @5 (first: u64, blocks: u64) -> (device: cap<Endpoint>) error Error;

    /// Where a traced request went in the provider (a request whose tag
    /// has bit 62 set is traced; the 16 most recent of the device's, any
    /// client's, are kept): when it was taken from the ring, when the
    /// device accepted it, and when it was completed (monotonic
    /// nanoseconds, the clock every program reads; 0: not yet). Only the
    /// client that made the request may ask, and a request's times are its
    /// own whatever tags other clients use; another client's traced
    /// requests can push it out of the 16.
    trace @6 (tag: u64) -> (taken: u64, started: u64, done: u64) error Error;
}
